<body>
    <div class="breadcrumb"><a href="../index.html">KC Tools Documentation</a> / Analysis</div>
    <div class="header-container">
        <div class="title-area">
            <h1>Workset Counts</h1>
            <p>Analyzes and displays element count by category for a selected workset</p>
            <p class="meta">Source command: <code>Cmds_Analyze_WorksetCounts</code></p>
        </div>
        <div class="plugin-icon">
            <div class="icon-box"><div class="icon-letter">WC</div></div>
            Workset<br>Counts
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="content-grid">
        <div class="main-column">
            <h2>What this tool does:</h2>
            <p>Analyzes a selected workset and displays the total element count broken down by category. This is useful for understanding the composition and scale of elements within a specific workset in a workshared model.</p>

            <h2>How to use the tool:</h2>
            <ol class="step-list">
            <li><strong>Start the command</strong><br>Click the Workset Counts button in the Revit ribbon.</li>
            <li><strong>Select a workset</strong><br>A dialog will appear listing all available worksets. Select the workset you want to analyze.</li>
            <li><strong>Wait for analysis</strong><br>A progress bar shows the analysis progress as the tool counts elements by category.</li>
            <li><strong>Review the results</strong><br>A report window displays the total element count and a breakdown by category, sorted from highest to lowest count.</li>
        </ol>

            <h2>Best use case:</h2>
            <div class="callout"><p>Use <strong>Workset Counts</strong> when you need to quickly understand the element distribution within a specific workset, diagnose workset imbalance, identify categories with excessive elements, or validate workset organization in a shared model.</p></div>

            <h2>Output information:</h2>
            <p>The tool displays:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Workset name:</strong> The name of the selected workset</li>
                <li><strong>Total elements:</strong> Complete count of all elements in the workset</li>
                <li><strong>Category count:</strong> Number of unique categories represented</li>
                <li><strong>Category breakdown:</strong> Sorted list showing element count per category</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="side-column">
            <h2>Requirements:</h2>
            <div class="requirement">
                <p><strong>Workshared Model Required</strong><br>This command only works with workshared Revit models. It will fail if run on a non-workshared document.</p>
            </div>

            <h2>Notes and tips:</h2>
            <div class="mini-card">
                <h3>Performance</h3>
                <p>Analysis speed depends on the total element count. Large worksets with thousands of elements may take a few moments to process.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="mini-card">
                <h3>Cancellation</h3>
                <p>You can cancel the analysis at any time by clicking the Cancel button on the progress bar.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="mini-card">
                <h3>No Categories</h3>
                <p>Elements without an assigned category will be grouped under <code>&lt;No Category&gt;</code> in the results.</p>
            </div>

            <h2>Command reference:</h2>
            <div class="mini-card">
                <p><strong>Category:</strong> Analysis</p>
                <p><strong>Command name:</strong> <code>WorksetCounts</code></p>
                <p><strong>Full source name:</strong> <code>Cmds_Analyze_WorksetCounts</code></p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
